Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and RICKMERS-LINIE GmbH & Cie. KG v. CS Marine Co., Ltd.
— dispute over compensation for damage from vessel collision

[Ruling Summary]

Article 208 of the Maritime Code of the People's Republic of China (hereinafter referred to as the Maritime Code) provides that claims for salvage remuneration are not subject to limitation of liability for maritime claims. However, when the salved party treats the salvage remuneration it has paid to a third party as its own loss and claims compensation from the opposing party in the collision, the nature of such remuneration transforms into damage caused by the collision accident, and the opposing party in the collision may enjoy the right to limit its liability for maritime claims with respect to such remuneration.

When applying the rule of "first set-off, then limitation" under Article 215 of the Maritime Code, if both non-personal injury claims and personal injury claims exist concurrently between the parties, the two different categories of claims shall be set off separately and limited separately.

Plaintiff (Counterclaim Defendant): Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG, domiciled at Neumühlen Street, Hamburg, Germany.
Legal Representative: Moritz Mittelbach and Holger Strack, General Managers of the company.

Plaintiff (Counterclaim Defendant): RICKMERS-LINIE GmbH & Cie. KG, domiciled at Neumühlen Street, Hamburg, Germany.
Legal Representative: Jan Boje Steffens, Director of the company.

Defendant (Counterclaim Plaintiff): CS Marine Co., Ltd., domiciled at Jungang-dong, Jung-gu, Busan, South Korea.
Legal Representative: Chang-Sub Lee, Chairman of the Board.

The plaintiffs, Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG (hereinafter referred to as Genoa Company) and RICKMERS-LINIE GmbH & Cie. KG (hereinafter referred to as Rickmers Company), filed a lawsuit with the Shanghai Maritime Court against the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., concerning a dispute over compensation for damage from a vessel collision.

The two plaintiffs alleged: Plaintiff Genoa Company is the registered owner of the Marshall Islands-flagged vessel "Rickmers Genoa" (hereinafter referred to as the "Genoa" vessel), and Plaintiff Rickmers Company is the bareboat charterer of the "Genoa" vessel. On March 8, 2005, the "Genoa" vessel collided with the South Korean-flagged vessel "Sun Cross" (hereinafter referred to as the "Sun Cross" vessel), which was bareboat chartered by the defendant CS Marine Co., Ltd., in the waters off Lianyungang. Subsequently, an explosion and fire occurred in cargo hold No. 1 of the "Genoa" vessel, causing huge damage to the hull and cargo, and resulting in the death of one crew member. The losses incurred include ship repair costs, salvage costs, fuel loss, loss of hire, crane costs, and compensation for personal injury and death of crew members, totaling USD 27,956,939.27. The plaintiffs argued that the "Sun Cross" vessel should bear 75% of the liability for the collision. Based on the defendant's proportional liability in the collision, the plaintiffs claimed compensation of USD 10,000,000, plus corresponding interest losses and litigation costs.

The defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., argued: It did not dispute the fact of the collision between the "Sun Cross" vessel and the "Genoa" vessel on March 8, 2005, or the plaintiffs' standing to bring the claim. However, a substantial portion of the USD 27,956,939.27 in losses claimed by the plaintiffs was not supported by sufficient evidence; most of the plaintiffs' losses were caused by the explosion and fire, which should be distinguished from the direct losses caused by the collision. The defendant further argued that the explosion and fire on the "Genoa" vessel after the collision were not directly caused by the collision, but were attributable to negligence on the part of the shipper and the plaintiffs in the custody of the cargo. The defendant claimed the right to limit its liability for maritime claims in accordance with the law, and submitted that its limit of liability should be 715,595 Special Drawing Rights (SDRs).

The defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., filed a counterclaim, alleging: Following the collision between the "Sun Cross" vessel and the "Genoa" vessel, the "Sun Cross" vessel sank and became a total loss. Except for two crew members who were rescued, all other crew members were lost. After the collision, the first port of arrival for the "Genoa" vessel was Shanghai Port. At the time of the collision, the defendant was the bareboat charterer of the "Sun Cross" vessel. After the collision, the owner of the "Sun Cross" vessel, Gasing Shipping Co., Ltd., assigned to the defendant all rights related to the accident (except for the subrogation rights exercisable by the vessel's insurer). The collision caused the defendant losses of USD 4,700,000, including loss of vessel value, loss of operating income, compensation for personal injury and death of crew members, and oil pollution cleanup costs. Based on the statements of the surviving crew members of the "Sun Cross" vessel after the collision and the investigation materials of the Maritime Safety Administration of the People's Republic of China, the defendant argued that the plaintiffs, Genoa Company and Rickmers Company, should bear 75% of the liability for the accident. The counterclaim sought a judgment ordering the plaintiffs to compensate for the economic losses of USD 3,530,000 caused by the accident, plus interest losses, and to bear the litigation costs.

The plaintiffs, Genoa Company and Rickmers Company, responded to the counterclaim filed by the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., arguing: Both parties were at fault in the collision, and since the defendant claimed the right to limit its liability for maritime claims regarding the collision, the collision losses should be apportioned and set off between the parties. As the plaintiffs' losses were greater than the defendant's losses, the defendant had no right to claim compensation from the plaintiffs.

Regarding the facts of the collision, the plaintiffs, Genoa Company and Rickmers Company, submitted the following evidence:

1.  Eleven pieces of evidence, including the "Genoa" vessel's classification certificate, safety management certificate, Marshall Islands tonnage certificate, international load line certificate, safe manning certificate, and crew competency certificates, to prove that the vessel's crew was properly manned and the vessel was seaworthy;
2.  Deck logs, engine room logs for March 3 to March 13, 2005, night orders for March 7, course records, engine telegraph records, etc., for the "Genoa" vessel, to prove the navigation status of the vessel at the time of the collision;
3.  Ten groups of evidence, including the bridge notice board, master's standing orders, compass log, voyage plan, pre-departure checklist, compass deviation table, stowage plan when leaving Shanghai, vessel loading condition, and long-term voyage records, to prove relevant information regarding the cargo loading for the voyage;
4.  Marine accident reports prepared by the master of the "Genoa" vessel on March 16 and 17, 2005, records of maritime investigation interviews with the master and crew, and three statements from the able seaman on watch of the "Sun Cross" vessel, to prove the circumstances of the collision.

Regarding the losses of the "Genoa" vessel, the plaintiffs, Genoa Company and Rickmers Company, submitted the following evidence:

1.  The final ship repair agreement signed between the "Genoa" owner and COSCO Nantong Shipyard on October 29, 2005, invoices and completion certificates issued by COSCO Nantong Shipyard, to prove the repair items and repair costs of the "Genoa" vessel amounted to USD 5,178,000;
2.  Fifty-seven groups of evidence, including invoices and payment vouchers for the repair of the "Genoa" vessel and the purchase of equipment, to prove payment of USD 4,814,271.26 for purchased equipment, materials, and repair costs for the Hamburg crane;
3.  Fifteen groups of evidence, including invoices for port charges at Shanghai Port and Nantong Port for the "Genoa" vessel, to prove additional port charges of USD 663,846.02 incurred due to the collision;
4.  The salvage contract with the Shanghai Salvage Bureau for the salvage of the "Genoa" vessel and payment vouchers, to prove that Plaintiff Genoa Company paid USD 4,522,428 to the Shanghai Salvage Bureau;
5.  The on-hire/off-hire certificate for the "Genoa" vessel, repair and delivery schedule, confirmation letter from the Shanghai Salvage Bureau, and fuel measurement records, to prove the fuel loss of the "Genoa" vessel was USD 171,716;
6.  Records of compensation paid to cargo owners of the "Sun Cross" vessel, bank transfer slips from Genoa Company to the cargo owners of the "Sun Cross" vessel, and confirmation letters from the cargo owners, to prove that Plaintiff Genoa Company actually paid USD 770,000 to the cargo interests of the "Sun Cross" vessel;
7.  Repair reports from Edmondson Company and invoices for crane rental costs incurred at the ports of Houston, Wilmington, Antwerp, and Tianjin, to prove that the collision caused damage to the crane in cargo hold No. 1 of the "Genoa" vessel, resulting in crane rental costs of USD 64,484.10;
8.  Letters and invoices from SHH Company, to prove that Plaintiff Genoa Company paid adjustment fees of USD 283,648.69 for particular average and operating loss;
9.  Remittance confirmations, receipts, and employment contracts for compensation paid by the owner of the "Genoa" vessel to the mother and relatives of the deceased chief officer, to prove compensation expenses of USD 132,814 resulting from the crew member's death;
10. Settlement agreements and bank payment slips between the "Genoa" vessel and Lianyungang Haisheng Ship Service Engineering Co., Ltd., and the East China Sea Fishery and Port Supervision Bureau of the Ministry of Agriculture, to prove payment of oil pollution cleanup costs of USD 621,178.19 and compensation for fishery resources loss of USD 48,000;
11. Invoices from Haiwen & Partners Law Firm, debit notes from Si-An Bank, invoices from Holman Fenwick & Willan Law Firm, invoices from Fowler Rodriguez, invoices from Chalos O'Connor, and survey fees and expert costs, to prove payment of legal costs of USD 1,793,734 and other costs of USD 711,156;
12. Investigation reports and supplementary reports dated March 27, 2007, and January 9, 2008, respectively, prepared by expert GRAHAM M CHARLTON of BURGOYNES (UK), to prove that the explosion and fire on the "Genoa" vessel were caused by the collision with the "Sun Cross" vessel;
13. Voyage profit calculation tables for five sister ships of the same type and specifications as the "Genoa" vessel before and after the collision, profit calculation table for the 144th voyage of the "Genoa" vessel before the accident, and charter hire reports from CLARKSON, to prove that the average daily loss of hire of USD 25,100 for the "Genoa" vessel during the repair period was reasonable.

Regarding the facts of the collision, the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., submitted the following evidence:

1.  Plain text fax from Lianyungang Maritime Safety Administration, to prove that the "Sun Cross" vessel sank due to the collision with the "Genoa" vessel;
2.  Certificate of nationality for the "Sun Cross" vessel, to prove that the owner of the vessel was Gasing Shipping Co., Ltd.;
3.  Bareboat charter party for the "Sun Cross" vessel, to prove that the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., was the bareboat charterer of the vessel;
4.  Letter of undertaking, bareboat charter party, civil ruling, and certificate of ownership for the "Genoa" vessel, to prove that Plaintiff Genoa Company was the owner of the "Genoa" vessel and Plaintiff Rickmers Company was the bareboat charterer of the "Genoa" vessel;
5.  Investigation report on the maritime traffic accident, to prove that the Zhejiang Maritime Safety Administration conducted a maritime investigation of the collision, and the cause of the accident was the fault of both vessels;
6.  Analysis and investigation report on the explosion from CWA Company, to prove that the cause of the explosion and fire on the "Genoa" vessel was that the shipper failed to correctly declare the hazardous nature of the cargo, improper stowage of the cargo, and the master's failure to take proper firefighting and other remedial measures;
7.  Answer and counterclaim filed by the owner of the "Genoa" vessel in the US litigation, to prove that in the US litigation, the owner of the "Genoa" vessel admitted that the explosion was caused by the shipper's failure to correctly declare the cargo, and the owner of the "Genoa" vessel claimed against the shipper in the US litigation.

Regarding the losses of the "Sun Cross" vessel, the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., submitted the following evidence:

1.  Rights assignment agreement, to prove that the owner of the "Sun Cross" vessel assigned all rights related to the accident to the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd.;
2.  Marine insurance policy and vessel valuation report for the "Sun Cross" vessel, to prove that the value of the "Sun Cross" vessel at the time of the accident was USD 3,900,000, and after deducting the insurance amount of USD 2,400,000, the loss of vessel value was USD 1,500,000;
3.  Time charter party for the "Sun Cross" vessel, to prove loss of hire of USD 332,450 suffered by the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., due to the collision;
4.  Wreck location survey contract, to prove that the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., paid KRW 300,000,000 for wreck location survey costs;
5.  Compensation agreements and payment details for personal injury and death of crew members, to prove that the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., paid a total of KRW 1,712,579,082 and USD 118,960 in compensation for the personal injury and death of the missing crew members;
6.  Settlement agreement for oil pollution cleanup costs and settlement agreement for compensation for damage to marine fishery resources, to prove that the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., compensated USD 570,000 for oil pollution cleanup costs and USD 60,000 to the East China Sea Fishery and Port Supervision Bureau for damage to marine fishery resources;
7.  Fuel cost details, to prove that the defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., suffered fuel loss of USD 34,397 due to the collision.

During the trial, upon the application of the parties, the Shanghai Maritime Court obtained relevant investigation materials from the Zhejiang Maritime Safety Administration. In addition, based on the vessel information for the "Sun Cross" vessel acknowledged by both parties, and upon commission by the court, Shanghai Sunsea Marine Development Co., Ltd. issued an appraisal report on March 12, 2009, concluding that the market price of the "Sun Cross" vessel in China on March 8, 2005, was USD 3,090,000.

The Shanghai Maritime Court held a public hearing of this case. During the hearing, regarding evidence on the facts of the collision: The defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., had no objection to the authenticity, legality, and relevance of the two plaintiffs' evidence materials, but argued that if certain contents of the said evidence materials were inconsistent with the investigation findings of the Zhejiang Maritime Safety Administration, the investigation findings of the Zhejiang Maritime Safety Administration should prevail. The plaintiffs, Genoa Company and Rickmers Company, had no objection to the authenticity of the form of the investigation report on the maritime traffic accident from the Zhejiang Maritime Safety Administration, but objected to the contents of the report; had no objection to the authenticity and legality of the analysis and investigation report on the explosion from CWA Company, noting that this evidence had been submitted by CS Marine Co., Ltd. to the Korean court, but the Korean court found a causal relationship between the collision and the explosion and fire on the "Genoa" vessel; had no objection to the authenticity of the answer and counterclaim submitted by the owner of the "Genoa" vessel in the US litigation, but argued that CS Marine Co., Ltd. could not be absolved of its liability in the collision due to the fault of the shipper of the "Genoa" vessel; had no objection to the other evidence submitted by CS Marine Co., Ltd. Regarding the relevant investigation materials obtained by the court from the Zhejiang Maritime Safety Administration, CS Marine Co., Ltd. confirmed the accident report and related materials, while the two plaintiffs only objected to the opinion in the accident report that the "Genoa" vessel took a hard-a-port helm order only 1.2 to 1.5 minutes before the collision, arguing that the "Genoa" vessel had taken an evasive action of turning to port 3 minutes before the collision and took a hard-a-port helm order 1 minute before the collision.

Regarding evidence on the losses of the "Genoa" vessel: The defendant, CS Marine Co., Ltd., argued that part of the evidence in Group 2 was irrelevant; that Group 6 evidence only proved that the plaintiffs paid USD 769,969.13 to JacksonParton, but did not prove that the payment was made to Tokyo Marine and Fire Insurance Co., Ltd., and since there was no settlement agreement signed with the cargo owners, the relevance of this expense could not be confirmed; that Group 7 evidence could not prove the relevance of these costs to the accident; that the general average adjustment costs in Group 8 evidence had no legal basis; that the legal costs in Group 11 evidence had no legal basis, but agreed to some reasonable expert fees; that the authenticity of Group 12 evidence was confirmed, but the purpose of proof was challenged; that the authenticity of Group 13 evidence was confirmed, but it did not meet the evidentiary requirements for loss of hire under the Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on Compensation for Property Damage in Cases of Trial of Cases concerning Vessel Collisions and Allisions. The defendant had no objection to the other evidence.

Regarding evidence on the losses of the "Sun Cross" vessel: The two plaintiffs had no objection to the authenticity of the above evidence. They agreed to entrust a third party, Shanghai Sunsea Marine Development Co., Ltd., to issue an appraisal report on the value of the "Sun Cross" vessel at the time of the accident and to accept the conclusion of that appraisal report. They challenged the reasonableness of the amount of loss under the time charter party and the amount of the wreck location survey contract costs.

The Shanghai Maritime Court found in its first instance examination:

At 10:36 on March 7, 2005, the "Genoa" vessel departed from Shanghai Port, bound for Qingdao Port. The vessel carried a total of 133 containers, weighing 2,728 tons; 7,696 pieces of general cargo, weighing 10,201 tons; and four passengers on board, including one German, one Dutch, and two Americans. At around 14:25, the "Genoa" vessel exited the Yangtze River estuary, and the pilot disembarked. At around 23:45, the second officer and the able seaman on watch successively reported to the bridge for watch duty. At 02:00 on March 8, 2005, the "Genoa" vessel was at latitude 34°27'.1 N, longitude 122°22'.0 E, gyrocompass course 317°, speed approximately 20 knots, visibility approximately 3 nautical miles. Two ARPA radars on the bridge were in operation, VHF radio was monitored on channel 16, navigational aids were in normal working order, and the vessel was on autopilot. At around 02:50, due to light fog, visibility reduced to 1.5 nautical miles. The second officer on watch did not call the master to the bridge as required by the night orders, nor did he take corresponding measures such as reducing speed or sounding fog signals. According to the statement of the second officer of the "Genoa" vessel, when the "Sun Cross" vessel was first detected on radar, the distance between the two vessels was 9 nautical miles, and the "Sun Cross" vessel was on the port bow of the "Genoa" vessel. At 8 nautical miles, the second officer considered there was a risk of collision, so he called the other vessel on VHF channel 16, but received no response. When the distance between the two vessels reduced to 1.5 nautical miles, the watch personnel of the "Genoa" vessel stated they saw the other vessel's green side light and established contact with the "Sun Cross" vessel via VHF. The watch personnel of the "Sun Cross" vessel requested that the "Genoa" vessel maintain its course and speed, which the "Genoa" vessel did. Two minutes before the collision, when the distance between the two vessels was 1 nautical mile, the "Genoa" vessel began turning to port, eventually to hard-a-port. At 03:03, the bow of the "Genoa" vessel struck the "Sun Cross" vessel near the port side wheelhouse. The collision position was approximately latitude 34°40'.4 N, longitude 122°05'.4 E. After the collision, cargo hold No. 1 in the bow of the "Genoa" vessel began to flood. The master ordered the closure of all hatches and vents of cargo hold No. 1. Over four hours later, the first explosion occurred in the hold, followed by several more explosions. Inspection reports showed that the explosions were caused by the reaction of seawater with magnesium desulfurization agent loaded in four containers in cargo hold No. 1, releasing hydrogen gas, which accumulated and was then ignited. The bow of the vessel was severely damaged by the explosions, and the chief officer of the vessel died in the explosions.

At 1400 hours on March 7, 2005, the vessel "Chongyang" departed from Lanshan Port in Jiangsu, China, bound for YAWATA Port in Japan. The voyage carried a total of 5,100 tons of pig iron, with a stern draft of approximately 6.5 meters at departure. According to the statement of the able seaman BAYI on duty on the "Chongyang," the course before the incident was 100°, speed 11.5 knots, autopilot engaged, foggy weather with visibility of approximately 1 nautical mile. At the time of the incident, the duty personnel on the bridge of the "Chongyang" were Second Officer KIM-OHOL-GON and BAYI himself; the Second Officer was responsible for lookout and vessel navigation, while BAYI was in charge of steering. The evasive action taken by the Second Officer of the "Chongyang" was first to apply 10° starboard rudder, followed immediately by hard-a-starboard. About 2 minutes after the hard-a-starboard, the collision occurred at approximately 0300 hours on March 8. The bow of the "Genova" struck the lifeboat deck on the port side of the bridge of the "Chongyang"; the angle of impact is unclear. Before the collision, the Second Officer on duty of the "Chongyang" had contacted the Second Officer on duty of the "Genova" via VHF. Following the collision, the "Chongyang" sank immediately. At the time of the incident, the vessel was fully laden, and 11 of the 13 crew members died.

The waters of the collision were open. At the time of the incident, the wind was southwest force 5 to 6, with gusts up to force 7, there was fog, the current was west-northwest with a slow velocity (less than 1 knot).

The collision caused total property losses to the "Genova" of RMB 5,060,254.63 and USD 20,547,844.72, and personal injury/death compensation losses of USD 115,000. The collision caused total non-personal injury property losses to the "Chongyang" of USD 1,894,397 and personal injury/death compensation losses of USD 1,369,999.02.

The owner of the "Genova" is the plaintiff Genova Company, and the bareboat charterer is the plaintiff Rickmers Company. The "Genova" was built in February 2004, a Marshall Islands-flagged multi-purpose vessel, with a gross tonnage of 23,119 tons, net tonnage of 9,752 tons, deadweight tonnage of 30,151 tons, and main engine power of 15,785 kW. On October 27, 2005, it was renamed "RICKMERS DAILIAN."

The owner of the "Chongyang" is Jiasheng Shipping Co., Ltd., and the bareboat charterer and manager is the defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd. On April 7, 2005, Jiasheng Shipping Co., Ltd. issued a letter of assignment confirming the transfer of all its rights related to the "Chongyang" and this incident to CS Shipping Co., Ltd. The "Chongyang" was built in June 1984, a South Korean-flagged general cargo vessel, with a gross tonnage of 3,785 tons, net tonnage of 2,163 tons, deadweight tonnage of 5,761 tons, and service speed of 13.5 knots.

It was further found that on April 7, 2004, the defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd. and Jiasheng Shipping Co., Ltd., as the insureds, entered into two hull insurance contracts with the insurer Dongbu Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd., with contract numbers 730030000492 and 730030000493. The two contracts indicated that the insured vessel was the "Chongyang," the insurance period was from 12:00 hours (Korea Standard Time) on March 24, 2004, to 12:00 hours (Korea Standard Time) on March 24, 2005, the subject matter insured was hull and machinery, and the insured amounts were USD 400,000 and USD 2,000,000 respectively. The co-insurance clauses of the above two insurance contracts specifically provided that Dongbu Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd. underwrote 69%, with an insured amount of USD 1,656,000; Dongyang Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd. participated in underwriting 21%, with an insured amount of USD 504,000; and Samsung Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd. participated in underwriting 10%, with an insured amount of USD 240,000. The insurance contracts also stipulated insurance clauses, deductibles, and premium rates. On April 1, 2005, pursuant to the claims demands of CS Shipping Co., Ltd. and Jiasheng Shipping Co., Ltd., Dongbu Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd., Meritz Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd. (formerly known as Dongyang Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd.), and Samsung Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd. paid a total insurance claim amount of USD 2,400,000. On April 7 of the same year, CS Shipping Co., Ltd. and Jiasheng Shipping Co., Ltd. issued a letter of subrogation to Dongbu Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd., Meritz Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd., and Samsung Fire & Marine Insurance Co., Ltd.

On March 12, 2009, based on the vessel documents for the "Chongyang" recognized by both parties, at the commission of the court, Shanghai Shuangxi Maritime Development Co., Ltd. issued an appraisal report, concluding that the market price of the "Chongyang" in China on March 8, 2005, was USD 3,090,000. After the trial, both parties, based on the gross tonnage of the "Chongyang" of 3,785 tons, confirmed that the limitation amount for maritime claims for non-personal injury of the vessel was 715,595 Special Drawing Rights, equivalent to USD 1,050,000.

The Shanghai Maritime Court held at first instance:

The main disputes among the parties in this case are: (1) the apportionment of liability for the vessel collision; (2) the causal relationship between the losses of the "Genova" and the collision; (3) whether the defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd.'s claim to enjoy limitation of liability for maritime claims according to law should be granted; (4) whether the non-personal injury claims and personal injury claims of both parties should first be consolidated and then set off.

(1) Regarding the apportionment of liability for the vessel collision

The two plaintiffs, Genova Company and Rickmers Company, argued that both vessels were proceeding at full speed before the collision and neither took reasonable collision avoidance measures, and thus at least both should bear 50% liability. The defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd. argued that based on the statements of the surviving crew members of the "Chongyang" and the materials from the investigation of the incident by the maritime authorities of the People's Republic of China, the two plaintiffs should bear 75% liability for the incident.

The court held that the collision occurred in open waters. From the time the risk of collision arose until the collision occurred, the navigational waters were areas of restricted visibility. The analysis of the faults of both parties should apply the relevant provisions of Section 1 and Section 3 of Chapter 2 of the *International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea, 1972* (COLREGS). Both parties bore equal responsibility and duty in their collision avoidance actions.

According to the statements of the duty crew members of both vessels, the collision time was approximately 0303 hours on March 8. Before the collision, the "Genova" was on a course of 317° at a speed of 20 knots; the "Chongyang" was on a course of 100° at a speed of 11.5 knots. The "Genova" had detected the "Chongyang" when the distance between the vessels was 9 nautical miles; from this, it can be calculated that the time until the collision was about 20 minutes. When the distance between the vessels was 8 nautical miles, the Second Officer of the "Genova" considered there was a risk of collision but only called the other vessel via VHF. Under foggy conditions with visibility of 1.5 nautical miles and without a response from the other vessel, the "Genova" did not take measures such as reducing speed, altering course to avoid collision, or sounding fog signals. The conduct of the "Genova" violated Article 6 of the COLREGS concerning "safe speed"; Article 8 concerning "any action taken to avoid collision shall, if the circumstances of the case admit, be positive, made in ample time and with due regard to the observance of good seamanship"; paragraphs 2, 3, and 4 of Article 19 concerning "every vessel shall proceed at a safe speed adapted to the prevailing circumstances and conditions of restricted visibility"; and Article 35 concerning "sound signals in restricted visibility." In addition, the duty crew of the "Genova" did not notify the captain to come to the bridge in foggy conditions as required by the captain's night orders. Furthermore, it was not until two minutes before the collision, when the distance between the vessels was 1 nautical mile, that the autopilot was switched to hand steering and a turn to port was executed until hard-a-port. This violated paragraphs 4 and 5 of Article 19 of the COLREGS concerning "if a vessel detects another vessel by radar alone, she shall determine if a close-quarters situation is developing and/or risk of collision exists. If so, she shall take avoiding action in ample time and, if such action consists of an alteration of course, so far as possible, avoid an alteration of course to port for vessels forward of the beam." Therefore, the crew of the "Genova" detected the "Chongyang" when they were 9 nautical miles apart but took no evasive action. Only two minutes before the collision, at a distance of 1 nautical mile, they executed a turn to port until hard-a-port, which clearly violated the COLREGS provision "avoid an alteration of course to port for vessels forward of the beam." Although the COLREGS contain a provision allowing departure from the rules to avoid immediate danger (the "departure rule"), the port turn taken by the "Genova" not only failed to achieve collision avoidance but, on the contrary, led to the occurrence of the collision. Therefore, the court held that the "Genova" should bear 60% liability for this incident.

According to the statements of the rescued able seaman of the "Chongyang" and the crew of the "Genova," before the incident the "Chongyang" was on course 100°, speed 11.5 knots, autopilot, foggy weather, visibility approximately 1 nautical mile, and it did not sound the appropriate fog signals. When the distance between the vessels was 1.5 nautical miles, the "Chongyang" contacted the "Genova," then applied 10° starboard rudder and hard-a-starboard, and the collision occurred two minutes later. Thus, the "Chongyang" proceeded at full speed throughout the foggy navigation, which did not comply with the COLREGS requirement that "every vessel shall at all times proceed at a safe speed," violating Articles 6 and 35 of the COLREGS. It took evasive action only about three minutes before the collision when the vessels were 1.5 nautical miles apart, and there was no evidence that the "Chongyang" took any action to reduce speed, failing to comply with the COLREGS requirements to "take action in ample time" and "if necessary, stop the vessel," violating Articles 8 and paragraph 5 of Article 19 of the COLREGS. In summary, the "Chongyang" failed to detect the oncoming vessel in a timely manner, proceeded at full speed throughout foggy navigation, and failed to take evasive action early. It should bear 40% liability for this incident.

(2) Regarding the causal relationship between the losses of the "Genova" and the collision

The defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd. argued that the explosion and fire on the "Genova" were not solely caused by the collision but also because the shipper failed to accurately declare the dangerous goods, the carrying vessel failed to properly stow and handle the dangerous goods, and the master failed to take correct measures after the collision. The two plaintiffs argued that the magnesium desulfurizer involved was not a dangerous good, the shipper did not declare it as such, and there was no problem with storing the cargo in No. 1 hold; the explosion and fire occurred solely as a result of the unexpected collision. The master was correct to maintain the airtightness of each compartment given that the collision had caused water ingress into No. 1 hold.

The court held that the explosion was caused by a large amount of seawater entering No. 1 hold of the "Genova" after the collision, which reacted with magnesium, the main component of the magnesium desulfurizer stowed in containers in that hold, producing hydrogen gas. Without the collision, the containers containing the magnesium desulfurizer would not have been submerged in seawater, and it would have been impossible for a large amount of seawater to permeate the containers and react with the magnesium desulfurizer to produce hydrogen and cause an explosion. Therefore, the argument that there was no causal relationship between the collision and the explosion is untenable. After the collision caused substantial water ingress into No. 1 hold, the master of the "Genova" closed all hatches and ventilators of No. 1 hold. In determining whether the master's salvage measures were negligent, the court held that the analysis should be based on the circumstances and conditions existing at the time of the collision. Any self-rescue action by the master, as long as it was not intentional or deliberately taken with knowledge that it would increase the loss, should be considered normal and reasonable.

(3) Regarding whether the defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd.'s claim to enjoy limitation of liability for maritime claims according to law should be granted

This case involves a collision damage dispute where both parties bear liability. The losses of the "Genova" amounted to over USD 20 million, while the "Chongyang" had a gross tonnage of only 3,785 tons. CS Shipping Co., Ltd. argued that it is entitled to limitation of liability for maritime claims under the Chinese Maritime Code, with a limitation amount of 715,595 Special Drawing Rights. The two plaintiffs raised no objection to this.

The court held that Article 204 of the Chinese Maritime Code provides that shipowners, including charterers and operators, may enjoy limitation of liability for maritime claims according to law. The defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd., as the bareboat charterer of the "Chongyang," qualifies as a person entitled to limitation of liability under the Chinese Maritime Code, and the two plaintiffs have no objection to this. Therefore, CS Shipping Co., Ltd. is legally entitled to limit its liability. Pursuant to Article 210 of the Chinese Maritime Code, the limitation amount for non-personal injury claims of the "Chongyang" is 715,595 Special Drawing Rights, and the limitation amount for personal injury claims is 1,844,405 Special Drawing Rights.

The claims of the two plaintiffs, Genova Company and Rickmers Company, for vessel repair costs, vessel equipment purchase costs, pollution cleanup costs, period charter losses, and other maritime claims fall under Article 207 of the Chinese Maritime Code, which concerns claims for loss of life or personal injury or damage to property occurring on board or in direct connection with the operation of the ship, or claims for other loss resulting from infringement of non-contractual rights occurring in direct connection with the operation of the ship or salvage operations. These are claims subject to limitation. Pursuant to that article, regardless of the manner in which the claims are brought, the defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd. may limit its liability. Additionally, the two plaintiffs claimed losses from the defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd. for salvage costs of USD 4,522,428. The court held that although this expense was triggered by the collision, the salvaged vessel was the "Genova," and there was a salvage contract relationship between the salvor and the two plaintiffs (the salvaged parties), governed by privity of contract. The salvor could only claim salvage remuneration from the two plaintiffs. With respect to CS Shipping Co., Ltd., it was not the salvaged party. The partial liability of CS Shipping Co., Ltd. for this salvage cost arises from the tort legal relationship of the vessel collision, not from a salvage contract. Therefore, for CS Shipping Co., Ltd., this amount is not salvage remuneration. Consequently, the claim by the two plaintiffs for reimbursement of this salvage cost loss falls under Article 207, item (3) of the Chinese Maritime Code, i.e., "claims for other loss resulting from infringement of non-contractual rights occurring in direct connection with the operation of the ship or salvage operations." CS Shipping Co., Ltd. may claim limitation of liability for this.

(4) Regarding whether the non-personal injury claims and personal injury claims of both parties should first be consolidated and then set off

Although the two plaintiffs, Genova Company and Rickmers Company, did not object to the defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd.'s claim for limitation of liability for maritime claims, they argued that, according to Article 215 of the Chinese Maritime Code, if a person entitled to limitation of liability makes a counterclaim against the claimant arising from the same incident, the claim amounts of both parties shall be set off against each other, and the limitation amount shall apply only to the balance between the two claim amounts. In other words, all losses of both parties should be set off together, and then CS Shipping Co., Ltd. may limit its liability. CS Shipping Co., Ltd., on the other hand, argued that the personal injury compensation claims and non-personal injury property compensation claims arising from this collision should be handled separately. That is, personal injury claims should be set off separately; non-personal injury property compensation claims should be set off against each other, and after the set-off, due to the substantial amount, CS Shipping Co., Ltd. may legally enjoy limitation of liability for maritime claims.

Based on the facts found, the vessel collision caused the two plaintiffs non-personal injury property losses of RMB 5,060,254.63 and USD 20,547,844.72, and payment of USD 115,000 for personal injury/death compensation to the chief officer of the "Genova." According to their respective liability proportions, the defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd. should compensate the two plaintiffs for 40% of these losses, i.e., non-personal injury property losses of RMB 2,024,101.85 and USD 8,219,137.89, and personal injury losses of USD 46,000. Similarly, the collision caused CS Shipping Co., Ltd. total non-personal injury property losses of USD 1,894,397 and payment of crew personal injury/death compensation of the "Chongyang" totaling USD 1,369,999.02. According to the liability proportions, the two plaintiffs should compensate CS Shipping Co., Ltd. for 60% of these losses, i.e., non-personal injury property losses of USD 1,136,638.20 and personal injury losses of USD 821,999.41. According to the plaintiffs' position, after the consolidated losses are set off, CS Shipping Co., Ltd. should compensate the two plaintiffs over USD 6 million, but because CS Shipping Co., Ltd. enjoys limitation of liability, the limitation amount is 715,595 Special Drawing Rights, equivalent to USD 1,050,000. According to CS Shipping Co., Ltd.'s position, the two categories of losses are set off separately: CS Shipping Co., Ltd. must compensate the two plaintiffs USD 1,050,000 for non-personal injury losses, and the two plaintiffs must compensate CS Shipping Co., Ltd. USD 775,999.41 for personal injury losses.

The court held that both parties in this collision were at fault and suffered losses. Therefore, when the defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd. claims limitation of liability for maritime claims, it should first set off claims and then apply the limitation, in accordance with Article 215 of the Chinese Maritime Code. Regarding the specific method of set-off, the Chinese Maritime Code provides different rules and limitation amounts for personal injury and non-personal injury maritime claims (Articles 210 and 211). From this, it can be inferred that the two types of claims should not be consolidated first and then limited, as this would make it impossible to apply the respective limitation provisions. Therefore, when both types of claims arise, they can only be set off separately and apply different maritime liability limitation amounts. Furthermore, Article 210, paragraph 1, item (3) of the Chinese Maritime Code also provides that "if the amount for claims for loss of life or personal injury is insufficient to pay such claims in full, the shortfall shall rank rateably with the claims for property damage," which further implies that Chinese law does not protect personal injury claims and non-personal injury claims in an identical manner. This difference is also reflected in the fact that a claimant for personal injury/death maritime claims may seek compensation from any party to the vessel collision. CS Shipping Co., Ltd.'s payment of personal injury/death compensation to its own crew exceeded its proportionate share of liability, and it is entitled to seek recourse against the two plaintiffs. Therefore, in this case, the non-personal injury compensation claims and personal injury compensation claims should be handled separately according to law. When CS Shipping Co., Ltd. applied for limitation of liability for maritime claims in this case, it argued for the application of the limitation amount for non-personal injury maritime claims, and the court accepts this pursuant to law.

In summary, the court held that the mutual recourse between the two plaintiffs, Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, and the defendant, CS Shipping Co., Ltd., should be divided into claims for personal injury compensation and claims for non-personal injury compensation, which should be offset separately in accordance with the law. With respect to the claims for non-personal injury compensation, since the total loss of the vessel "Genoa" less the total loss of the vessel "Chongyang" (including the insurance indemnity of USD 2,400,000 already paid by the vessel's hull insurer) was substantially greater than the tonnage limitation limit for the vessel "Chongyang", CS Shipping Co., Ltd. was entitled to limit its liability for maritime claims under the law and to pay the two plaintiffs the sum of 715,595 Special Drawing Rights. With respect to the claims for personal injury compensation, after offsetting, the two plaintiffs were still required to pay CS Shipping Co., Ltd. the amount of USD 775,999.41 in personal injury compensation.

As for the interest claimed by both parties on the losses, at the trial both parties agreed that it should be calculated at the demand deposit rate of the People's Bank of China (annual rate 0.72%) from March 9, 2006 to February 24, 2009. The court considered that, for reasons of fairness and convenience, it was not improper to calculate the interest on the various losses from one year after the date of the collision to the date of the trial, and the court could support this. With regard to the interest on the limitation amount for maritime claims applied for by the defendant CS Shipping Co., Ltd., the two plaintiffs, Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, claimed that it should be calculated at the demand deposit rate of the People's Bank of China (annual rate 0.72%) from the date of the incident until the effective date of this judgment. The court held that since CS Shipping Co., Ltd. had not actually applied to the court to constitute a limitation fund, the plaintiffs' claim was in accordance with the law, and the court supported it.

In summary, in accordance with Articles 93 and 94 of the Special Maritime Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, Article 64, Paragraph 1 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and Articles 165, 169, 204, 207, 208, 210, Paragraphs 1 and 2, 212, 215, 252, and 256 of the Maritime Code of the People's Republic of China, the Shanghai Maritime Court rendered the following judgment on July 30, 2009:

1. The defendant, CS Shipping Co., Ltd., shall, within ten days from the effective date of this judgment, compensate the plaintiffs, Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, for property losses caused by the ship collision in the amount of USD 1,050,000, together with interest on that amount (calculated at the annual demand deposit rate of the People's Bank of China of 0.72% from March 9, 2005 to the effective date of this judgment).

2. The plaintiffs, Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, shall, within ten days from the effective date of this judgment, compensate the defendant, CS Shipping Co., Ltd., for personal injury losses caused by the ship collision in the amount of USD 775,999.41, together with interest on that amount (calculated at the annual demand deposit rate of the People's Bank of China of 0.72% from March 9, 2006 to February 24, 2009).

3. The other claims of the plaintiffs, Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, are rejected.

4. The other counterclaims of the defendant, CS Shipping Co., Ltd., are rejected.

Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, dissatisfied with the first-instance judgment, appealed to the Shanghai High People's Court, requesting that Item 1 of the original judgment be amended, and that the judgment be altered to order CS Shipping Co., Ltd. to compensate Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company for property losses and personal injury losses of USD 1,050,000 plus interest; and requesting that Item 2 of the original judgment be revoked, and that the judgment be altered to order that Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company no longer pay any separate personal injury loss compensation to CS Shipping Co., Ltd. The main grounds were: 1. The original judgment improperly determined the proportion of collision liability, finding that the vessel "Genoa" bore 60% of the accident liability without sufficient basis. In conditions of restricted visibility, the failure to proceed at a safe speed meant the faults of both vessels were equal; when the vessels were in sight of one another, the provisions of Section II, Chapter 2 of the International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea, 1972 should apply, and the vessel "Chongyang" should bear greater liability; the original judgment's finding that the "Genoa's" port turn measure was improper and led to the collision was without factual basis. 2. The original judgment erred in separately offsetting the personal injury compensation claims and the non-personal injury compensation claims of the two parties and applying different tonnage limitation limits. The original judgment erroneously confused the parties' claims for recourse against each other for personal injury payments with the "personal injury compensation claims" stipulated in the Maritime Code; even if the "claims for personal injury losses" raised by the two parties were considered "personal injury compensation claims" under the Maritime Code, these claims should be settled together with all other claims within the same limitation limit. 3. Since the original judgment held that the personal injury claims and non-personal injury claims should be offset and limited separately, it should also have held that the claims for salvage costs raised by Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company against CS Shipping Co., Ltd. should be offset separately and not subject to limitation.

CS Shipping Co., Ltd. argued in defense that the contention of Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company that the collision liability of both parties was equal lacked basis. According to the maritime investigation, both before and during the collision, visibility was restricted. Therefore, the collision avoidance rules for restricted visibility should apply to this case, not the rules for vessels in sight of one another. There was no causal relationship between the explosion on the "Genoa" and the collision; there was a direct causal relationship between improper stowage of cargo and the explosion. If the cargo, magnesium desulfurizer, had been stowed on deck, the collision would not have led to the explosion. Furthermore, the improper conduct of the master and crew was also one of the causes of the explosion. Regarding the operating loss of the "Genoa," the evidence submitted by Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company consisted only of tables prepared unilaterally and an appraisal commissioned unilaterally, which were not objective, so the evidence was insufficient.

CS Shipping Co., Ltd. also appealed, requesting that Item 1 of the original judgment be revoked, and that the judgment be altered to order Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company to jointly and severally compensate CS Shipping Co., Ltd. for non-personal injury losses of USD 420,000 plus interest; and that Item 2 of the original judgment be revoked, and that the judgment be altered to order Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company to jointly and severally compensate CS Shipping Co., Ltd. for personal injury losses of USD 1,027,499.25 plus interest. The grounds were: 1. The original judgment improperly determined the proportion of collision liability between the two vessels; for this collision incident, the "Genoa" should bear 75% of the liability, not the 60% determined by the original judgment. 2. The original judgment failed to distinguish between the explosion losses and the collision losses of the "Genoa"; CS Shipping Co., Ltd. should not be liable for the explosion losses of the "Genoa," including the personal injury of the "Genoa's" chief officer. 3. The operating loss of the "Genoa" of USD 6,174,600 was not proven by sufficient and reliable evidence submitted by Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, and therefore should not have been recognized.

Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company responded, arguing that the faults of both parties in the collision were equivalent, and that CS Shipping Co., Ltd.'s request for them to bear 75% of the collision liability lacked basis; the original judgment's finding on the causal relationship between the collision and the explosion of the "Genoa" was correct; the original judgment's finding that the operating loss of the "Genoa" was USD 6,174,600 was reasonable and correct. Accordingly, they requested that CS Shipping Co., Ltd.'s appeal be dismissed.

The Shanghai High People's Court, upon second instance, confirmed the facts found in the first instance.

The Shanghai High People's Court held in the second instance as follows:

The issues in dispute in this case are: How should the proportion of collision liability be apportioned? Is there a causal relationship between the explosion of the "Genoa" and the collision? In the limitation of liability, how should the property losses and personal injury losses of both parties be offset?

Regarding the operating loss of the "Genoa." In the original trial, both parties confirmed that the "Genoa's" voyage delay was 246 days. As for the standard for calculating the operating loss per day of voyage delay, the appellants, Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, provided the voyage profit calculation tables for five sister ships of the same type and specifications before and after the collision, the profit calculation table for the 144th voyage of the "Genoa" before the incident, and the CLARKSON charter report. Although Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company failed to provide evidence of the average net profit for the two voyages of the "Genoa" before and after the collision, they did provide evidence that the average daily charter hire for a vessel of the same type and specifications as the "Genoa" was USD 25,100 and evidence from internationally published materials that the average daily charter hire for vessels similar in type and specifications to the "Genoa" from March 2005 to January 2006 was greater than USD 25,100. Therefore, the original judgment's calculation of the operating loss of the "Genoa" during the voyage delay period based on a daily charter hire of USD 25,100 had a factual basis and was relatively reasonable, and the court confirmed this. The appellant, CS Shipping Co., Ltd., failed to provide evidence to prove that this calculation standard was clearly excessive or unreasonable, so the court did not accept its factual assertion disputing the original judgment's determination of the "Genoa's" operating loss.

Regarding the proportion of liability. This collision occurred in waters with restricted visibility. Both parties to the collision were at fault for failing to navigate with caution, use a safe speed, and take early evasive action. However, the "Genoa" took a port turn, eventually to hard-a-port, two minutes before the collision when the vessels were one nautical mile apart. This measure clearly violated the provision of the International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea, 1972 regarding "avoiding an alteration of course to port for a vessel forward of the beam," and this led to the collision. On this basis, the original judgment's finding that the "Genoa" bore the primary fault for the collision and should bear 60% of the accident liability had a factual and legal basis. The court did not support the claims of the appellants, Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, that the faults of the two vessels were equal or that the "Chongyang" should bear greater liability, nor the claim of the appellant, CS Shipping Co., Ltd., that the "Genoa" should bear 75% of the liability for this incident, as they all lacked factual basis.

Regarding the causal relationship between the collision and the explosion of the "Genoa." The explosion of the "Genoa" occurred because, after the collision, a large amount of water entered No. 1 cargo hold of the "Genoa," which chemically reacted with magnesium, the main constituent of the magnesium desulfurizer stowed in the containers in that hold, releasing hydrogen gas. The explosion of the "Genoa" was caused by the combination of the collision, which led to water ingress into the cargo hold, and the chemical reaction between that water and the shipboard cargo, releasing hydrogen. In this chain of causation, the ship collision was the initiating cause, and the water ingress into the cargo hold and the chemical reaction with the cargo were the conditions; the explosion resulted from the combination of these factors. Without the collision, the condition of water ingress into the cargo hold and the chemical reaction with the shipboard cargo could not have arisen to cause the explosion. Therefore, there is a legal causal relationship between the explosion of the "Genoa" and the collision in question. The appellant CS Shipping Co., Ltd.'s appellate argument that there was no causal relationship between the explosion of the "Genoa" and the collision lacked basis and was not accepted. As for whether the master's action, after the collision caused extensive flooding in No. 1 cargo hold, of closing all hatches and ventilators of that hold aggravated the possibility of the explosion and constituted fault, the court agreed with the view of the original trial court: "The determination of whether the master's salvage measures were negligent should be analyzed based on the environment and conditions existing at the time after the collision. Any act of self-preservation by the master, unless it is intentional or known to increase the loss, should be considered normal and reasonable."

Regarding the issue of how to offset the property losses and personal injury losses of both parties within the limitation of liability—specifically, whether to offset by category (property vs. personal injury) for both parties and then aggregate, or to aggregate all property and personal injury losses of each party together before offsetting the total amounts. According to Article 210 of the Maritime Code, claims for personal injury compensation and claims for non-personal injury compensation in the limitation of liability for maritime claims are two different types of maritime claims. The per-unit limitation amounts for these two types of claims are also different; the per-unit limitation amount for personal injury claims is significantly higher than that for non-personal injury claims, almost double. Furthermore, the Maritime Code also prescribes different priority orders for satisfaction within the limited maritime liability, with personal injury claims taking precedence over non-personal injury claims. Based on this, the court held that the original judgment's understanding that "the two types of claims can only be offset separately and subject to different tonnage limitation limits" is more consistent with the legislative intent of the Maritime Code. The court approved the original judgment's approach of offsetting the personal injury claims and non-personal injury claims in this case by category, and then applying different types of tonnage limitation limits. The court did not accept the proposition of the appellants, Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, that the personal injury claims and non-personal injury claims in this case should each first be aggregated before limiting liability, as it lacked legal basis.

In summary, the original judgment found the facts clearly and applied the law correctly, and should be affirmed. The appellate claims of the appellants, Rickmers Genoa Schiffahrtsgesellschaft mbH & Cie. KG and Rickmers Company, and the appellate claims of the appellant, CS Shipping Co., Ltd., all lacked factual and legal basis, and the court did not support them. Accordingly, in accordance with Article 153, Paragraph 1, Item (1) and Article 158 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Shanghai High People's Court rendered the following judgment on June 16, 2011:

Appeals dismissed, and the original judgment affirmed.

This judgment is final.
